    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-of-belgium"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-i-of-belgium.jpg" alt="Leopold I of Belgium" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-of-belgium">Leopold I of Belgium</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NICAISE_Leopold_ANV.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicaise de Keyser, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> arose from the southern provinces of the United <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> had joined to the north in 1815 under William I. Catholics resented a Protestant king’s control of education, liberals resented his autocratic style, and the south felt under-represented in the army and administration and burdened by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> language policy and taxes. On 25 August 1830 a performance of the opera <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-muette-de-portici">La Muette de Portici</a></em> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> sparked riots; in the September Days Dutch troops failed to retake the city, and on 4 October a provisional government declared independence. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-congress-belgium">National Congress</a> adopted a constitution on 7 February 1831 and, after the great powers vetoed a French prince, chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-i-of-belgium">Leopold of Saxe-Coburg</a>, a German prince who had been the widower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>’s heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-charlotte-of-wales">Princess Charlotte</a>. He took the oath on 21 July 1831, now the national day. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Conference of the great powers recognised Belgian independence, and French armies twice protected the new state: against the Dutch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ten-days-campaign">Ten Days’ Campaign</a> of August 1831 and at the siege of the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> in 1832. William I finally accepted the separation in the Treaty of London of 1839, which fixed the frontiers, divided Limburg and Luxembourg, and guaranteed Belgium’s perpetual neutrality.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/seraing"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seraing.jpg" alt="Seraing" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/seraing">Seraing</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seraing_-_Casa_de_la_vila.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">flamenc, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> was the first country on the European continent to industrialise on the British model. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">Coal</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borinage">Borinage</a>, the Centre and the Liège basin fed ironworks such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-cockerill">John Cockerill</a>’s at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seraing">Seraing</a>, the textile mills of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verviers">Verviers</a>, and later glass, zinc and engineering industries. The state built a national railway network, opening the line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> to Mechelen in 1835, the first steam passenger railway in continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/societe-generale-de-belgique">Société Générale</a> and other banks financed industry at home and abroad. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a> grew into one of the great ports of Europe after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheldt">Scheldt</a> toll was bought off in 1863. Prosperity was unequal: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> suffered a severe crisis in the 1840s when its linen industry collapsed and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> blight struck, and industrial workers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallonia">Wallonia</a> lived in harsh conditions that erupted in the strikes and riots of 1886. Society was divided between Catholics and anticlerical liberals and later socialists, organised into rival ‘pillars’ of parties, unions, schools and cooperatives. The language question shaped everything: French dominated government, courts, education and the elite, while most of the population spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> dialects in Flanders. In the twentieth century economic weight shifted from declining Walloon heavy industry to Flanders, with its port, chemicals and services, reversing the old balance between the regions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saio"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-saio.jpg" alt="Battle of Saïo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saio">Battle of Saïo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belgian_forces_with_captured_Italian_artillery.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>’s neutrality, guaranteed in 1839 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, defined its military position for most of the nineteenth century. Its main works were fortifications: the national redoubt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antwerp">Antwerp</a>, built from the 1850s, and the ring forts of Liège and Namur designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-alexis-brialmont">Henri Alexis Brialmont</a> in the 1880s. In August 1914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> demanded passage for its armies, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-i-of-belgium">King Albert I</a>’s government refused, Germany invaded on 4 August, bringing Britain into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. The forts of Liège delayed the Germans for some days before falling to heavy siege artillery, and the German advance was marked by massacres of civilians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dinant">Dinant</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a>, where the university library was burned. The Belgian army held the last corner of the country behind the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yser">River Yser</a> from October 1914 until the liberation offensive of 1918, while the rest of Belgium endured four years of occupation. After the war Belgium abandoned neutrality and allied with France, then returned to an independent policy in 1936. On 10 May 1940 Germany invaded again; after eighteen days King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-duke-of-austria">Leopold III</a> surrendered the army on 28 May, against the wishes of his ministers, who continued the war from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. Belgian troops also fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, notably at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saio">Saïo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1941, and a Belgian battalion served in the Korean War.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-i-of-belgium"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-i-of-belgium.jpg" alt="Albert I of Belgium" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/albert-i-of-belgium">Albert I of Belgium</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Albert_I_of_Belgium_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Richard Speaight, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The constitution of 1831 was one of the most liberal of its time, establishing a hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, ministerial responsibility, a two-chamber parliament and guarantees of freedom of religion, the press, education and association; it became a model for constitutions elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Voting rights were at first restricted to a small number of taxpayers. Parliamentary politics was dominated by the Catholic and Liberal parties, which clashed over schools, notably in the ‘school war’ of 1879–84, and the Belgian Workers’ Party, founded in 1885, campaigned for democracy. A general strike in 1893 forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-suffrage">universal male suffrage</a> with plural votes for the wealthy and educated, replaced in 1919 by equal male suffrage; women gained the vote in national elections in 1948. The kings held real influence over foreign and military affairs. Leopold I (1831–1865) was a respected European diplomat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> (1865–1909) pursued colonial ambitions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-i-of-belgium">Albert I</a> (1909–1934) became a national hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-duke-of-austria">Leopold III</a> (1934–1951) provoked the ‘Royal Question’ by his conduct in 1940. He was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baudouin-of-belgium">Baudouin</a> (1951–1993), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-ii-of-belgium">Albert II</a> (1993–2013) and Philippe, king since 2013. From 1970 a series of state reforms divided the unitary state into language communities and regions, and in 1993 the constitution declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> a federal state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/eupen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eupen.jpg" alt="Eupen" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/eupen">Eupen</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evening_view_of_Sankt_Nikolaus_from_Loten,_Eupen,_2023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DimiTalen, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>’s influence far exceeded its size. Its constitution of 1831 inspired liberal constitutions across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and its early railways and industrial firms spread technology and capital abroad, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Its colonial rule had the gravest consequences. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-ii">Leopold II</a> obtained recognition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> as his personal possession in 1885; its regime of forced labour for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, enforced by violence and mutilation, caused enormous loss of life and provoked the international Congo reform movement, until Belgium annexed the territory as the Belgian Congo in 1908. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> Belgium gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eupen">Eupen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malmedy">Malmedy</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a> of Ruanda-Urundi, where its administration hardened the distinction between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> and Tutsi. After 1945 Belgium became a builder of the new Europe: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-henri-spaak">Paul-Henri Spaak</a> was a leading architect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-rome">Treaty of Rome</a>, Belgium co-founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benelux">Benelux</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Communities</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> became the main seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-commission">European Commission</a> and the Council and, from 1967, of NATO’s headquarters. Belgian culture has been prolific, from the painters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ensor">James Ensor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-magritte">René Magritte</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/art-nouveau">Art Nouveau</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-horta">Victor Horta</a> to the comics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herge">Hergé</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolphe-sax">Adolphe Sax</a> invented the saxophone and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georges-lemaitre">Georges Lemaître</a> proposed the theory of the expanding universe.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-duke-of-austria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-duke-of-austria.jpg" alt="Leopold III, Duke of Austria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-duke-of-austria">Leopold III, Duke of Austria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leopold_III_of_Austria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Antoni Boys, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> has not ended, but it has been transformed from a unitary, French-dominated monarchy into a federal state of language communities. The Flemish Movement won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> equal status in law in 1898, a Dutch-speaking university at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghent">Ghent</a> in 1930 and the language laws of 1932, and the language frontier was fixed in 1962–63. The Royal Question divided the country along the same lines: in the referendum of 1950 a majority voted for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopold-iii-duke-of-austria">Leopold III</a>’s return, carried by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> but rejected in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallonia">Wallonia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>, and after violent protests he abdicated in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baudouin-of-belgium">Baudouin</a> in 1951. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decolonisation">Decolonisation</a> was abrupt: the Congo became independent on 30 June 1960 and descended into crisis, and Rwanda and Burundi followed in 1962. The splitting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-university-of-leuven">Catholic University of Leuven</a> in 1968 and the division of the national parties along linguistic lines led to the state reforms of 1970, 1980, 1988–89 and 1993, which created the Flemish, French and German-speaking communities and the regions of Flanders, Wallonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels-capital-region">Brussels-Capital</a>; further reforms followed in 2001 and 2011–14. Forming governments became increasingly difficult, most notably after the 2010 election, when the country went 541 days without a new government. Today Belgium is a federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> of about 11.8 million people under King Philippe, a founding member of the euro area.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, created by revolution in 1830 and guaranteed as neutral by the great powers, became continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s first industrial nation, ruled the Congo through one of the most notorious colonial regimes, endured two German occupations and turned its divisions between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> and French speakers into a federal state at the heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>.</em></p>

